{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline\/wavyLine","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"symbolKind":"property","externalID":"s:7IvorMXL10MXLBarlineV8wavyLineAA07MXLWavyE0VSgvp","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"wavyLine","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL11MXLWavyLineV","text":"MXLWavyLine","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"title":"wavyLine","roleHeading":"Instance Property"},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline"]]},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A wavy line associated with the barline, such as for a trill or vibrato that continues across it."}],"sections":[],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["macOS"],"tokens":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"wavyLine","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL11MXLWavyLineV","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLWavyLine","text":"MXLWavyLine","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","level":2,"text":"Discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Maps to a nested MusicXML ","type":"text"},{"code":"<wavy-line>","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" element."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbarline\/wavyline"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLBarline/wavyLine":{"title":"wavyLine","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline\/wavyLine","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"A wavy line associated with the barline, such as for a trill or vibrato that continues across it.","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"wavyLine"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"MXLWavyLine","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL11MXLWavyLineV"},{"kind":"text","text":"?"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbarline\/wavyline"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLWavyLine":{"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlwavyline","role":"symbol","title":"MXLWavyLine","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLWavyLine","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLWavyLine","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"Wavy lines are one way to indicate trills and vibrato. When used with a barline element, they should always","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"have type=“continue” set. The smufl attribute specifies a particular wavy line glyph from the SMuFL Multi-","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"segment lines range.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLWavyLine","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLBarline":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLBarline"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLBarline"}],"title":"MXLBarline","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"If a barline is other than a normal single barline, it should be represented by a barline type that describes"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"it. This includes information about repeats and multiple endings, as well as line style. Barline data is on"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the same level as the other musical data in a score — a child of a measure in a partwise score, or a part in a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"timewise score. This allows for barlines within measures, as in dotted barlines that subdivide measures in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"complex meters. The two fermata elements allow for fermatas on both sides of the barline (the lower one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"inverted)."}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbarline"}}}